a.failure_scenario:
A consumer follows MIGRATION.md and migrates their `Transaction` implementation to the surviving name:

    @Override public void signInOrder(SequencedCollection<Signer> s) { auditLog(s); Transaction.super.signInOrder(s); }

`myTx.signInOrder(signers)` runs the override (:845 -> :846 -> abstract `sign(SequencedCollection)`).
`myTx.signInOrder(blockHash, signers)` does NOT: :855 -> :856 calls `sign(recentBlockHash, signers)` at :801, whose body is `setRecentBlockHash(...); sign(signers);` -> the abstract seam. The override never runs and the transaction is signed with no audit record. Same for `signInOrderAndBase64Encode(signers)` (:850 -> :851 -> `signAndBase64Encode(SequencedCollection)` at :791 -> `sign(signers)`), `signInOrder(String, ...)` (:860), and both blockhash Base64 variants (:865, :870). Only 1 of 6 entry points sees the override.
The identical move on the by-key side works: overriding `signByKey(Collection)` at :883 IS honoured by :888, :894, :900, :906 and :912, because those funnel through it. Nothing at the declarations says the two families differ, and the one-line javadocs at :854/:859/:864/:869 never name the method they delegate to.
Then, when the deprecated members at :791/:801/:811/:822/:833 are removed, those five bodies must be re-pointed at `signInOrder(SequencedCollection)` — at which point the consumer's override starts running on all six paths. That is a behaviour change delivered BY the removal, which is exactly what a deprecation cycle exists to avoid.
